\section{Leave-one-out, and newdata}{

The weight of a draw for observation \code{i} comes from that observation's
own column of \code{\link[frmtmb:log_lik]{frmtmb::log_lik()}}, so a leave-one-out expectation exists
only for a row the model was fitted on. brms passes \code{newdata} to both
\code{loo()} and the prediction, and so computes the weights on the new
rows' own response. Here \code{\link[frmtmb:loo]{frmtmb::loo()}} and \code{\link[frmtmb:log_lik]{frmtmb::log_lik()}} refuse
\code{newdata}, and these functions refuse it in the same words, with
\code{re_formula} and the new-level arguments. For a summary on other
rows, use \code{\link[=posterior_predict]{posterior_predict()}} or \code{\link[=posterior_epred]{posterior_epred()}} with
\code{newdata}: on a row the model has not seen, there is no observation
to leave out.
}

\section{Draws}{

\code{ndraws} and \code{draw_ids} choose the draws ONCE, and the same draws give
the weights and the prediction. brms draws a random subsample for
each of the two, so its weights then belong to other draws than the
prediction they weight. As in \code{\link[frmtmb:loo]{frmtmb::loo()}} and brms, the relative
efficiency is taken on the chain structure when every draw is used,
and on one chain for a subset (\code{ndraws} or \code{draw_ids}). Draws from
\code{frm_sample(thin =)} keep their chains.
}

\section{Multivariate models}{

Without \code{resp}, the weights come from the joint \code{\link[frmtmb:log_lik]{frmtmb::log_lik()}}
of the responses, and the result has a third dimension with one layer
per response, named as brms names the responses (\code{ya} for a column
\code{y_a}). With one response in \code{resp}, the weights come from that
response's own log-likelihood, as brms's \code{loo(resp =)} computes
them, and the result is that response's matrix. With several, the
weights come from \code{\link[frmtmb:log_lik]{frmtmb::log_lik()}} of those responses (their
joint density under \code{set_rescor(TRUE)}, the sum of their densities
without it), and the result has one layer per response named, in
that order, as in brms.
}

